  nonce: eb8f  # eb8f
  data:  # eb8f
    kind: invariant  # eb8f
    title: "Per-pass provenance record's field set does not carry the data consumers need"  # eb8f
    status: violated  # eb8f
    priority: 1  # eb8f
    parent:  # eb8f
    tags:  # eb8f
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # eb8f
    - dogfood_integrated  # eb8f
    - dogfood_severity_significant  # eb8f
    isbefore: []  # eb8f
    duplicate_of: []  # eb8f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # eb8f
    pr_ref:  # eb8f
    description: "The per-pass provenance record (`behavior_map.analysis_runs[]`, 84 entries on hypergumbo self-analysis) carries a fixed set of fields meant to describe each pass's execution. Six of those fields fail in distinct ways: one is empty for the entire analyzer subset, one identifies the host interpreter rather than the analyzer toolchain, one is named as if it grouped by invocation but functions as a per-pass key, one is missing productivity counters entirely, one accounts for only half of total wall-clock, and one carries values in two incompatible format scales across passes. The record's downstream consumers (diagnostic dashboards, productivity reports, cache-invalidation logic that joins by pass key) cannot trust any of these fields to mean what their name implies.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `pass_version` uniformly empty for every language analyzer (44/44), populated for every linker (40/40).** Of 84 entries, 44 have empty `pass_version` and 40 carry a proper `sha256:<hex>`. The split is perfectly along producer-class lines: all 44 language analyzers (python, go, java, javascript, rust, bash, etc.) emit empty; all 40 linkers (containment-linker, http-linker, decorator-dispatch-linker, etc.) emit correct hashes. Root cause: `compute_pass_version` is invoked by `register_linker` but not by `register_analyzer`. The CHANGELOG claim — *\"AnalysisRun.pass_version via code-hash. compute_pass_version returns sha256 of the pass module source, replacing the fake -v1 suffix that bumped on every release regardless of logic changes\"* — is violated for the analyzer half of the records. Cache invalidation cannot fall back to per-analyzer code-hash for that cohort; the full `repo_fingerprint` carries the load instead. (Note: `enclosure-linker` is in the empty group despite the name — worth confirming whether it's actually wired through `register_linker` or `register_analyzer`.)\n\n**(2) `toolchain` hardcoded to `{name: \"python\", version: \"3.12.3\"}` for all 84 passes regardless of analyzer.** The bash analyzer's `toolchain` should record bash version or tree-sitter-bash grammar version; the JS analyzer should record its parser version. Current value reflects the host Python interpreter and is useless for cross-tool reproducibility. Compounds (1): even for the linker subset where `pass_version` works, `toolchain` is wrong.\n\n**(3) `execution_id` is unique per pass (84 UUIDs for 84 runs), not per CLI invocation.** It cannot group passes that came from the same `hypergumbo` invocation — it functions as a per-pass UUID identical in role to `run_signature`, not as a per-invocation group key. The name is misleading: schema-doc consumers would assume `execution_id` groups runs into invocations. The actual per-invocation grouping signal is not carried anywhere in the record.\n\n**(4) Roughly 58s (51%) of total wall-clock is non-pass overhead with no line item in `analysis_runs`.** Total wall-clock 1:54 (114s); cumulative pass duration 92.8s; pass wall-clock first-start to last-finish 55.96s. The missing ~58s presumably includes framework discovery, file index build, embedding cache priming, schema validation, JSON serialization, gitleaks invocation (if installed), and repo fingerprinting — none of which have `AnalysisRun`-style provenance entries. Consumers asking \"where did the time go\" cannot answer from the record. Implied parallelism during the pass phase is only 1.66× (max 6 concurrent passes despite 18 active languages), suggesting an additional scheduling-visibility gap. Compounds (1): even tracked passes only account for half of wall-clock.\n\n**(5) `AnalysisRun` lacks `nodes_emitted` / `edges_emitted`; per-pass productivity is reverse-derived only.** The 15 declared fields are: `config_fingerprint`, `duration_ms`, `execution_id`, `failed_files`, `files_analyzed`, `files_skipped`, `pass`, `pass_version`, `repo_fingerprint`, `run_signature`, `skipped_passes`, `started_at`, `toolchain`, `version`, `warnings`. None of: `nodes_emitted`, `edges_emitted`, `output_size_bytes`. Answering \"pyffi-linker emitted how many edges\" requires scanning all 108,649 edges checking origin lists for `pyffi-linker` — a multi-million-element filter operation per query. The data is computable from elsewhere but not curated on the record where productivity dashboards would naturally look. Direct fields would also make dead-pass detection trivial.\n\n**(6) `AnalysisRun.version` inconsistent: 83 runs use semver `5.0.1`, 1 (`play-routes`) uses `1`.** Same field carries two scales without schema-level distinction. Both runs have identical `toolchain={\"name\": \"python\", \"version\": \"3.12.3\"}`. Either `play-routes` was developed against a `v1` schema-version stamp and never updated to track the project semver, or the `version` field has different semantics per analyzer (project-semver for the main set, internal-revision for analyzers). No central version-stamping policy enforces consistency.\n\n### Invariant\n\nEvery field declared in the `AnalysisRun` schema should carry the semantics its name implies. `toolchain` should reflect the analyzer's actual toolchain (not the host interpreter). `pass_version` should be a code-hash for every pass-producer class (analyzer OR linker), not only linkers. Per-invocation grouping should be a different key from per-pass deterministic identity (`execution_id` shouldn't double as `run_signature`'s role). Per-pass productivity (`nodes_emitted`, `edges_emitted`) should be a first-class field on the record, not reverse-derivable only by scanning the full output. The non-pass-overhead wall-clock that constitutes ~half of every invocation should have its own provenance entries. Version-bearing fields should follow one scheme across all producers.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects every per-pass record (84/84 on hypergumbo self-analysis). The six distinct field-level symptoms each affect different subsets — 44/44 for `pass_version`-empty, 84/84 for `toolchain`-hardcoded and `execution_id`-misnamed, 1/84 for `version`-scale-drift — but the underlying issue is uniform: the provenance record's field set was specified without enforcing producer-side consistency, and producers drifted independently.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`behavior_map.analysis_runs[]` records, consumed by diagnostic and productivity dashboards, cache-invalidation logic that joins by `pass_version` or `run_signature`, schema-conformance gates, and any downstream tooling that filters or groups by provenance fields.\n\n### Consequence\n\nDiagnostic and productivity analyses based on the provenance record require ad-hoc rescanning of the full output for derived facts, inherit incorrect toolchain attribution, and cannot group passes by invocation. Cache invalidation cannot fall back to per-analyzer code-hash for 44 of 84 passes. Half of the total wall-clock is unaccounted for at the provenance level. Cross-pass version-stamping drift (one out of 84 carrying a different scale) means schema-validation cannot enforce field-level format invariants on the existing data.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Wire `compute_pass_version` into `register_analyzer` (the same way it's currently called from `register_linker`); verify `enclosure-linker`'s registration path.\n- Have each analyzer record its actual toolchain — `tree-sitter-<lang>` grammar version, language-specific parser version, etc. — instead of the host Python interpreter. Adopt a per-analyzer convention rather than a single hardcoded value.\n- Rename `execution_id` to reflect its actual per-pass-UUID semantics (or, alternatively, change its emission to be per-invocation and add a separate per-pass key with a clearer name). Document the per-invocation grouping convention in the schema.\n- Add `AnalysisRun`-style entries for non-pass overhead (framework discovery, file index build, schema validation, etc.) so the 58s gap is visible at the same granularity as pass durations.\n- Add `nodes_emitted` and `edges_emitted` fields to `AnalysisRun`. Aggregate them at orchestration time (cheaper than reverse-deriving per query).\n- Centralize version-stamping policy so all analyzers use one scale. Either project semver across the board, or per-analyzer revision across the board, but not a mix.\n- Add a provenance-completeness CI gate that snapshots every declared field on every record type in produced behavior maps and fails on any field that's documented in schema but unpopulated, wrong-typed, or format-inconsistent across records.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-vijij, INV-fuhup, INV-hukap, INV-nilid, INV-kimut — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # eb8f
    fields:  # eb8f
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-c96c76"  # eb8f
      statement: "Toolchain values should reflect the analyzer's actual toolchain. Per-invocation grouping should differ from per-pass keys. Version-bearing fields should follow one scheme. Per-pass productivity (node count, edge count) should be carried in the provenance record, not reverse-derived."  # eb8f
      root_cause: "The per-pass provenance record carries a fixed set of fields meant to describe a pass's execution. Multiple fields fail to capture what their name implies. One field meant to identify the analyzer toolchain (compiler/parser version) is hardcoded to the host Python interpreter version regardless of which language the analyzer parses. One field meant to group passes by invocation is emitted with a unique value per pass, so it behaves identically to the per-pass deterministic key and cannot be used to group passes by the single user invocation that produced them. One field meant to carry a pass version is empty for a substantial fraction of pass records. The record carries no per-pass node-count or edge-count, so answering \"how productive was each pass\" requires re-scanning the full edge list per pass. One field's value is emitted in one format for almost every pass and a different format for one pass."  # eb8f
